{-
   The operations a machine has and the IR does not - `lib/Native/Memory.native.yana`'s block moves
   and its system call, cut down to the declarations `compiler/resolve_mir` implements.

   All three are `@builtin`: what the compiler supplies is `mir::Node::Native`, and the signatures
   here are the signatures - see `compiler/resolve_mir/native.def`, where the three rows are, and
   `compiler/intrinsic/op.def`, where their shapes are.

   `lib/`'s `copyMemory` is a *body* - a vector ladder that calls `blockCopy` only for the lengths
   that pay a `rep movsb`'s startup back - and none of that is here: this stage has no vectors, so
   what remains is the thing the ladder bottoms out in.
-}

{-
   How many bytes a page is - `@builtin(pageBytes)`, so the zero is what the declaration must write
   and the target is what it holds.

   A supplied global rather than a call: it is a constant for the whole run, and a program that reads
   it in a loop should pay nothing. The initializer says nothing and has to be `0`, which
   `checkSuppliedGlobal` refuses to let a file lie about.
-}
@builtin(pageBytes)
pub let pageBytes = 0 :: Size

@builtin(blockCopy)
pub fn blockCopy(to: %U8, from: %U8, count: Size) -> {}

@builtin(setMemory)
pub fn setMemory(to: %U8, value: Size, count: Size) -> {}

{-
   The system call, at each arity a call needs.

   Seven declarations and one operation, because a call's argument count is what decides which
   registers the kernel reads and there is no variadic form to write.

   **The arguments are `USize` and the result is `Size`**, which is not an inconsistency: an argument
   is a word the kernel reads however the call says to - an address, a count, a flag word - and none
   of those is a number with a sign. The result is the one position where the kernel imposes a
   reading, being the answer where it is non-negative and the negated errno where it is not.
-}
@builtin(syscall0)
pub fn syscall0(number: USize) -> Size
@builtin(syscall1)
pub fn syscall1(number: USize, a: USize) -> Size
@builtin(syscall2)
pub fn syscall2(number: USize, a: USize, b: USize) -> Size
@builtin(syscall3)
pub fn syscall3(number: USize, a: USize, b: USize, c: USize) -> Size
@builtin(syscall4)
pub fn syscall4(number: USize, a: USize, b: USize, c: USize, d: USize) -> Size
@builtin(syscall5)
pub fn syscall5(number: USize, a: USize, b: USize, c: USize, d: USize, e: USize) -> Size
@builtin(syscall6)
pub fn syscall6(number: USize, a: USize, b: USize, c: USize, d: USize, e: USize, f: USize) -> Size
